at means a white pawn has reached the 7th rank! That’s huge — White has a pawn on d7, which is one step away from promotion. Black’s pieces are somewhat uncoordinated because king on e6 is exposed.\n\nEvaluation: The most critical feature is the passed white pawn on d7, ready to promote next move unless captured. Black’s pieces are not immediately attacking it: the knight on c6 is pinned by bishop b5 to the king on e6? Actually bishop b5 attacks c6 knight, but knight is defended by king on e6? Yes, king on e6 is adjacent to c6? No, e6 to c6 is two squares away diagonally? Wait coordinates: c6 is file c rank 6, e6 is file e rank 6, they are two squares apart horizontally, not adjacent. So knight is not defended by king directly. Black’s pieces that can capture d7 pawn: queen on d8? No queen is at d8? Board shows rank 8: a8 RBF0, b8 empty, c8 empty, d8 QBF0, e8 empty, f8 empty, g8 NBF0, h8 empty. So black queen is on d8, which attacks d7 pawn. Also rook on h7? Rank 7 h7 is RBF1, which is far. Also bishop on d6 attacks d7? Bishop on d6 is BBF1 at d6? Actually rank 6 d6 is BBF1, yes black bishop on d6 attacks d7 pawn. So pawn on d7 is attacked by queen and bishop, but it’s a white pawn, meaning it’s our pawn? Yes PWF1. So it’s our pawn on d7, defended? Possibly by bishop on b5? Bishop b5 attacks c6 but not d7. It’s not defended by any white piece currently. So pawn is hanging. But it’s on d7, if we can defend it or promote with check, that could be decisive.\n\nValid moves: We have many moves, but we must consider how to support d7 pawn or create threats that distract black from capturing it. Key candidate moves:\n1. **B: b5-c6** — attacking knight, which is defended by king? Actually knight on c6 is defended by king on e6 (king can move to d6 or d5 to recapture? But king defends knight only if knight is adjacent or within one square? King on e6 is two squares away, so not defending c6 knight. So Bxc6+ wins a knight and gives check. Black must move king (since no piece 