p to d3? Bb5-d3 defends d7 pawn? Actually d3 is two squares away from d7, not defending. We could move queen? We don’t have queen yet. Rook? Rooks are on a1 and h1. We could move Rf1 to defend d7? No, too far.\n8. **Check threat:** We have B: c1-g5 attacking queen? Bishop from c1 to g5 attacks queen on d8? No, g5 is not on same line as d8. Actually queen on d8 is on d8, g5 is far. So not.\n\nGiven the pinned pawn on d7 is a huge asset if we can unpin it. The pin is due to bishop on d6. So if we can attack that bishop or block the pin, we can free the pawn. For example, we could play Bc4 attacking f7 but not helping. Or we could play Nc3 then Nd5 attacking bishop? Nd5 would attack bishop on d6, but knight from c3 to d5 is two moves away. Not immediate.\n\nBetter: We can give a check that forces black to move king, potentially disrupting the pin. For instance, Bxc6+ forces king to move. If king moves to d6, then bishop on d6 is still there? Actually bishop on d6 is still there, pin remains. If king moves to e7, then bishop still there. So pin remains.\n\nWhat about moving our king to break the pin? If we move king to e2, then pawn is no longer pinned because king moves out of the line. But moving king to e2 might be risky because it’s exposed. However, if we move K e1-e2, then pawn on d7 is free to move. But after Ke2, black can capture d7 pawn with queen or bishop. So we need to defend pawn first. Could we play Ke2 and also defend d7? Not immediately.\n\nAlternatively, we can block the pin by placing a piece on d2. We have moves: N: b1-d2, or B: c1-d2. B: c1-d2 blocks the diagonal from bishop to king. That seems promising. If we play B: c1-d2, then the bishop on d6 no longer sees the king because our bishop blocks. Then pawn on d7 is unpinned and can promote next move. But after Bd2, black might capture d7 pawn immediately with queen or bishop. However, if we block with bishop, the pawn is still undefended. So black could capture it. But if black captures, we might recapture with something? For example, if Qxd7, then we have Bxd7? But bishop on d2 is not attacking d7. We could recapture with bishop from b5? Bishop b5 attacks d7? No, b5 to d7 is diagonal? b5 to d7: file difference 2, rank difference 2, yes it’s diagonal, so bishop b5 attacks d7. So if black queen takes d7, we can recapture with Bxd7, winning queen for bishop. But black might instead capture with bishop Bxd7, then we recapture with Bxd7 (bishop from b5), and we win bishop for bishop, but we lose the pawn. Still, we would have traded pawn for bishop, and we have a bishop pair? Not clear.\n\nBut maybe black has a better move after Bd2, like moving queen to safety and then capturing pawn. We need to calculate.\n\nAnother blocking move: N: b1-d2 also blocks the diagonal. Knight on d2 blocks the pin as well. Then pawn is free. And knight defends b3 and f3, but not d7. Still, after Nd2, black can capture d7 with queen or bishop. But then we might have tactics: if Qxd7, then Nc4 attacking queen? Knight from d2 to c4 attacks queen on d7? Yes, knight from d2 to c4 attacks d7. So queen would be attacked. But queen could move away, we then recapture with something? Actually if Qxd7, then we have Nc4 attacking queen, queen moves, we have lost pawn. But we might gain tempo. However, black could also play Bxd7, and then we have no immediate recapture because knight doesn’t attack d7 (knight on d2 attacks b3, f3, e4, c4, not d7). So we lose pawn for nothing.\n\nSo blocking with bishop seems better because bishop on d2 might later support something, and bishop b5 attacks d7.\n\nLet’s evaluate B: c1-d2:\n\nAfter Bd2, black has several options:\n- Capture d7 with queen: Qxd7. Then we capture with bishop b5: Bxd7. Then black recaptures with king? King on e6 cannot capture on d7 because d7 is adjacent? e6 to d7 is one square diagonally, yes king can capture. So Kxd7. Then we have lost two bishops for queen? Wait: we had bishop b5 and bishop d2. After Qxd7, we play Bxd7 (bishop from 