*R: e6-b6**, because after Rxb5?, Black’s rook on b8 is pinned (pawn on b4 pins to king? Not exactly pinned but attacked). Actually, RxP is not immediate because b5 pawn is defended by b4 pawn. But the rook move to b6 puts pressure on both b-pawns and eyes Black’s rook on b8.\n\n**Reason:**  \nR: e6-b6 increases White’s control and ties Black down. If Black moves the b-pawn, White may capture or penetrate. If Black tries to defend b5 with …a6, then Black’s rook is even more passive. White can later double rooks or activate the bishop. This move is most aggressive without risk (rook is safe on b6). It also keeps initiative, which is crucial for White to exploit Black’s cramped king and doubled pawns.\n\n**Answer:** R: e6-b6'}
